{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"biological-bleary-annelida","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context\n\nEpic `77e3-28e2` AC4 requires the code-review `tests` criterion to carry a taxonomy\ndistinguishing behaviour tests from tests that pin **refactor mechanics, re-export\nidentity, LOC floors, import shape, or re-run a gate script** — five named kinds.\n\nStory `f86e-b57b-008d-4652` delivered one of them (`loc-ceiling-pinning`). The taxonomy is\n2 of 5.\n\n## Measured at `origin/main` `0e7401446c5`\n\n`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` carries 11 antipattern entries:\n`change-detector`, `tautological`, `over-mocking`, `under-mocking`, `source-grepping`,\n`existence-only`, `non-gating test`, `unrealistic fault injection`,\n`contract-contradicting assertion`, `fail-open oracle`, `loc-ceiling-pinning`.\n\nMapping AC4's five named kinds onto those entries:\n\n| AC4 kind | Covered by | State |\n|---|---|---|\n| refactor mechanics | `change-detector` (\":58\" \"would break on a behavior-preserving refactor\") | covered |\n| LOC floors | `loc-ceiling-pinning` (f86e) | covered |\n| re-export identity | — | **absent** |\n| import shape | — | **absent** |\n| re-run a gate script | — | **absent** |\n\nCase-insensitive counts over the rubric: `re-export` 0, `reexport` 0, `__all__` 0,\n`import` 0, `gate script` 0, `re-run` 0, `rerun` 0. The three kinds are not named under any\nsynonym. `change-detector` subsumes them generically (\"asserts an internal name, structure,\nor intermediate state\"), but AC4 requires the taxonomy to *distinguish* them, and a reviewer\ngiven only the generic entry has no detection language for these three shapes.\n\n## Why the verification route is the eval corpus, not a prose grep\n\nAC4's \"Checked by\" clause names \"an overlay test asserting the taxonomy text is present in\nthe criterion's rubric\". The rubric's own Rule-5 structural-artifact exception (`:97-103`)\nforbids exactly that test:\n\n> Litmus: the grepped token is a real contract iff a NON-HUMAN consumer (a parser /\n> validator / downstream grep / registry / CI runner that branches on the exact value)\n> reads it. An LLM is NOT a non-human consumer — a prose/heading grep that merely guards\n> the author's wording IS a change-detector.\n\nMeasured: nothing but the LLM reads this rubric's antipattern headings. `git grep` over\n`src/ scripts/ tests/` finds the rubric referenced only by `criteria_routing.json` (a prose\n`_comment`), by `eval_specs/*.yaml` (which carry the text as fixture diffs), and by its own\nfront matter. No parser branches on the entry names.\n\nSo a test asserting the taxonomy's wording would commit the change-detector antipattern the\nrubric defines. The project has already established the substitute twice — the\n`criteria_routing.json:112` `_comment` records it for both `3fce` (fail-open oracle) and\n`f86e` (loc-ceiling-pinning):\n\n> Detection language + FP guards live in reviewers/code-review-tests.md; calibration corpus\n> in eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml (R-loc-ceiling-* / FP-loc-* cases).\n\nThis story follows that route: the taxonomy's correctness is demonstrated by calibration\ncases that show it FIRES on real instances and does NOT fire on the lookalikes, which is a\nstronger claim than its prose matching a fixed string.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 — `code-review-tests.md` carries a **re-export identity** antipattern entry:\n      a test whose assertion is that a symbol is importable from, or re-exported by, a\n      module (the identity of the re-export) rather than the behaviour reached through it.\n      The entry names the detection shape and at least one false-positive guard.\n- [ ] AC2 — it carries an **import shape** entry: a test asserting a module's import list,\n      `__all__` contents, or import ordering rather than behaviour. Guard: a genuine\n      public-API surface census that a non-human consumer reads is NOT this antipattern.\n- [ ] AC3 — it carries a **gate-script re-run** entry: a test that invokes the gate script\n      under test and asserts its output, duplicating the gate rather than exercising the\n      unit. Guard: Rule-5 command-output greps with an established producer-liveness anchor\n      remain valid.\n- [ ] AC4 — `eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml` gains recall cases (`R-reexport-*`,\n      `R-importshape-*`, `R-gatererun-*`) and false-positive guard cases\n      (`FP-reexport-*`, `FP-importshape-*`, `FP-gatererun-*`) for each new entry,\n      matching the naming convention the `3fce` and `f86e` cases use.\n- [ ] AC5 — the eval spec scores at or above its recorded pass bar, reproducible from the\n      committed spec.\n- [ ] AC6 — `criteria_routing.json`'s `_comment` records the three additions in the same\n      form it records `3fce` and `f86e`, naming where detection language and corpus live.\n      No threshold or `applies_to` change.\n- [ ] AC7 — `make verify` exits 0.\n\n## Portability and provider neutrality\n\nThe deliverable is rubric prose plus eval-corpus cases. Nothing reads a CI environment, so\nthis behaves identically with no CI provider configured. No vendor or model-specific\ncapability is named, and the entries are detection language the reviewer applies regardless\nof which provider backs the gate.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Changing the `tests` criterion's threshold or posture (already blocking at 0.54).\n- Amending epic 77e3's AC4 \"Checked by\" clause — that is the epic's own edit, justified by\n  the Rule-5 evidence above, and is not this story's deliverable.\n- The other two AC4 kinds, which are already covered.","id":"e508-ce6c-2e52-4adc","parent_id":"77e3-28e2-31ec-47ec","priority":2,"tags":["complexity-reduction"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Add the three missing code-review test-taxonomy entries: re-export identity, import shape, and gate-script re-run"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793748425751929448,"uuid":"72ea328f-202b-4903-8a0c-a3c8b619e79e"}